Linux commands– for any DevOps
      interview !🐧

Here is a complete list of essential Linux commands that every DevOps Engineer should know and practice.

File and Directory Management
pwd – Show the current working directory
ls – List files and directories
ls -l – List files with detailed information
ls -a – Show hidden files
cd <dir> – Change directory
mkdir <dir> – Create a new directory
rmdir <dir> – Remove an empty directory
rm -rf <dir> – Remove a directory with all its contents
cp file1 file2 – Copy files
mv file1 file2 – Move or rename a file

File Viewing and Editing
cat file – Display file contents
less file – View file page by page
head -n 10 file – Show the first 10 lines of a file
tail -n 10 file – Show the last 10 lines of a file
tail -f file – Monitor a file in real time (useful for logs)
nano file / vim file – Edit a file

File Permissions
ls -l – View file permissions
chmod 755 file – Change file permissions
chown user:group file – Change file ownership

Process Management
ps aux – List all running processes
top – Monitor system processes
htop – Interactive process viewer
kill -9 <pid> – Terminate a process by its ID
systemctl status service – Check service status
systemctl start/stop/restart service – Manage services

Disk and Memory Management
df -h – Show disk space usage
du -sh <dir> – Show the size of a directory
free -h – Show memory usage
uptime – Show system load and uptime

Networking
ifconfig / ip addr – Display network interfaces and IP addresses
ping <host> – Test network connectivity
curl <url> – Fetch data from a URL
wget <url> – Download a file from a URL
netstat -tulnp / ss -tulnp – Display listening ports
scp file user@host:/path – Securely copy a file to a remote server
ssh user@host – Connect to a remote server

User Management
whoami – Show the current user
id – Show user and group IDs
adduser <username> – Add a new user
passwd <username> – Change a user’s password
su - <user> – Switch to another user
groups – Display groups of the current user

Package Management

Debian / Ubuntu
apt update – Update package list
apt install <pkg> – Install a package
apt remove <pkg> – Remove a package

RHEL / CentOS
yum install <pkg> – Install a package
yum remove <pkg> – Remove a package

Logs Management
cd /var/log – Go to the log directory
tail -f /var/log/syslog – View system logs (Debian/Ubuntu)
tail -f /var/log/messages – View system logs (RHEL/CentOS)

Archiving and Compression
tar -cvf archive.tar files/ – Create a tar archive
tar -xvf archive.tar – Extract a tar archive
gzip file – Compress a file
gunzip file.gz – Decompress a file

Search and Find
find /path -name filename – Find files by name
grep "text" file – Search for text in a file
grep -r "text" /dir/ – Search text recursively in a directory
history – Display command history
